      "text": "We start with the equation $\\sec x + \\tan x = \\frac{4}{3}.$\nMultiply both sides by $\\sec x$ to get $\\sec^2 x + \\sec x \\tan x = \\frac{4}{3} \\sec x.$\nWe can rewrite this as $\\sec^2 x + \\sec x \\tan x = \\frac{\\sec^2 x - \\sec x \\tan x}{\\sec x}.$\nSimplifying further, we have $4 \\sec x = \\frac{\\sec^2 x}{\\sec x}.$\nThis implies that $\\frac{\\sec^3 x - \\sec x \\tan x}{\\sec x} = 4.$\nDividing both sides by $\\sec x,$ we get $\\sec^2 x - \\sec x \\tan x = 4.$\nSubstituting back $\\sec x = \\frac{1}{\\cos x},$ we have $\\frac{1}{\\cos^2 x} - \\frac{\\sec x \\tan x}{\\cos x} = 4.$\nSimplifying, we have $1 - \\tan x = 4 \\cos x.$\nUsing the Pythagorean identity $\\sin^2 x + \\cos^2 x = 1,$ we have $\\sin^2 x + \\cos^2 x - \\tan x = 4 \\cos x.$\nSubstituting back in $\\tan x = \\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x},$ we have $\\sin^2 x + \\cos^2 x - \\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x} = 4 \\cos x.$\nSimplifying further, we have $\\sin x (\\sin x + \\cos x - \\frac{4}{\\cos x}) = 0.$\nOne solution is $\\sin x = 0.$\nThe other two solutions are obtained by solving the equation $\\sin x + \\cos x - \\frac{4}{\\cos x} = 0.$\nMultiplying both sides by $\\cos x,$ we have $\\sin x \\cos x + \\cos^2 x - 4 = 0.$\nUsing the identity $\\cos^2 x = 1 - \\sin^2 x,$ we have $\\sin x \\cos x + 1 - \\sin^2 x - 4 = 0.$\nSimplifying, we have $\\sin x (\\cos x - \\sin x) = 3.$\nSince $\\cos x = \\sqrt{1 - \\sin^2 x},$ we have $\\sin x (\\sqrt{1 - \\sin^2 x} - \\sin x) = 3.$\nFactoring out $\\sin x,$ we have $\\sin x (\\sqrt{1 - \\sin^2 x} - \\sin x - 3) = 0.$\nOne solution is $\\sin x = 0,$ so the other two solutions are\n$\\sqrt{1 - \\sin^2 x} - \\sin x = 3.$\nWe can give two solutions in terms of complex numbers for the second one, but I leave the \"work\" in.",
