      "text": "Given that $\\sec x + \\tan x = \\frac{4}{3},$ we will find the possible values of $\\sin x$ using trigonometric identities and solving equations.\n\nStep 1: Recall the trigonometric identities:\n$\\sec x = \\frac{1}{\\cos x}$ and $\\tan x = \\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}$\n\nSo, $\\sec x + \\tan x = \\frac{1}{\\cos x} + \\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x} = \\frac{1 + \\sin x}{\\cos x} = \\frac{4}{3}$\n\nStep 2: Solve the equation\nWe have $\\frac{1 + \\sin x}{\\cos x} = \\frac{4}{3}$. Cross multiply to get $3(1 + \\sin x) = 4 \\cos x$.\n\nStep 3: Use $\\sin^2 x + \\cos^2 x = 1$:\n$(3(1 + \\sin x) - 4 \\cos x)(3(1 + \\sin x) - 4 \\cos x)$ and $\\sin^2 x + \\cos^2 x = 1$\nWe get a quadratic equation in terms of $\\sec x$ or $\\tan x$ which will help us get all possible angles for $x$\n\nStep 4: Solve the quadratic equation:\n$3(1 + \\sin x) - 4 \\cos x = -\\frac{1 + \\sin x}{3}$\n\nFinally, we will solve it:\n\n$9\\sin x - 4 \\cos x + 15 = 0 \\ldots(1)$\n\nNow using $\\sin^2 x + \\cos^2 x = 1$\n\nWe get the quadratic in terms of $\\sin x$ or $\\cos x$:\n\n$4 \\sin^2 x - 5 \\sin x + 3 = 0$\n\nNow, solving this quadratic equation we get the value for $\\sin x$ to be $1/2$ and $3/4$\n\nThus, the possible values are $\\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{3}{4}}$",
